    {
      "id": "turn_pending_1790010398017",
      "question": "What are the main regional patterns in the destinations of U.S. agricultural exports?",
      "origin": "qa-session",
      "suggested": true,
      "createdAt": 1790010408537,
      "turn": {
        "answer": "For Export share percentage, the regional distribution ranks North America first at 28.0%, followed by High income Asia and Pacific at 20.0%, Europe and Central Asia at 11.0%, South America and Caribbean at 10.0%, Middle East and North Africa at 5.0%, Sub-Saharan Africa at 2.0%, and South Asia at 1.0%. [G9] North America is the maximum and South Asia is the minimum in the 2011-15 agricultural export share of value outer ring, at 28% and 1%, respectively. [G161] [G108] The three largest regions in the Export share percentage series—North America, High income Asia and Pacific, and Europe and Central Asia—sum to 59.0%. [G9] The smallest destinations are in South Asia and Africa, with South Asia at 1.0% and Sub-Saharan Africa at 2.0%, while Middle East and North Africa accounts for 5.0%. [G9] A separately named export share of value profile shows High income Asia and Pacific at 33.0%, North America at 22.0%, Europe and Central Asia at 20.0%, and Middle East and North Africa at 6.0%. [G15] [G164] Within that four-region profile, High income Asia and Pacific is the largest destination and Middle East and North Africa is the smallest. [G15] Overall, the evidence shows differing regional contribution profiles, with the largest shares concentrated in North America and High income Asia and Pacific and the smallest shares in South Asia, Sub-Saharan Africa, and Middle East and North Africa. [G9] [G15]",
